            "answer": 1,
            "transcript": "Test Two.\nI am going to give you the instructions for this test. I shall introduce each part of the test and give you time to look at the questions. At the start of each piece you will hear this sound:\ntone\nYou will hear each piece twice.\nRemember, while you are listening, write your answers on the question paper. You will have five minutes at the end of the test to copy your answers onto the separate answer sheet.\nThere will now be a pause. Please ask any questions now, because you must not speak during the test.\n[pause]\nNow open your question paper and look at Part One.\n[pause]\nYou will hear people talking in eight different situations. For questions 1 to 8, choose the best answer (A, B or C).\nQuestion 1\nYou hear a girl telling a friend about a family trip she went on to a climbing centre.\n[pause]\ntone\nBoy: How was your climbing centre trip?\nGirl: Well, we hadn't studied the brochures very carefully so we weren't sure what to expect – we thought we'd enjoy being surprised when we got there. There was an enormous tower to climb up and then swing down! It did look fun, but it was much bigger than I thought it'd be and I didn't fancy trying it. Everyone else in my family loved it though – in fact, it eventually developed into them all seeing who could be the best at everything. I really wasn't keen on that. Luckily there were some smaller climbing walls too, so I had a go at those.\nBoy: Ah, right.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 2\nYou hear a teacher talking to her class about a visit to a science exhibition.\n[pause]\ntone\nWoman: As you know, we're off to the science exhibition next week, the theme of which is fire and light. There'll be demonstrations throughout the day, so we'll check the programme of events to see which ones will be the most interesting – educationally I mean. For example, there's one on what a candle needs in order to keep burning, which we covered in class last week, so take your notes to look at, then write down any additional observations. There's also a display about what happens to light when it hits different objects – hopefully you'll recall that from the homework you were asked to do. Any questions?\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 3\nYou hear two students discussing a film they saw on TV.\n[pause]\ntone\nBoy: Did you see that film on TV last night? The one about a girl who was adopted by the politician and his wife.\nGirl: Yeah I did, it was unusual and I liked that. It was a bit weird in places, though. I never imagined the girl would have such an impact on the couple who adopted her.\nBoy: Or that her adoptive parents would behave the way they did in the end. I thought the actor who played the girl was brilliant. But I wasn't convinced the director made a wise decision about the actors who played the parents.\nGirl: They're both famous though, so you can understand why he did it.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 4\nYou hear a woman talking about her work as a fashion designer.\n[pause]\ntone\nWoman: The first piece of clothing I remember altering was a vest I wore in high school. We had a dress code which we had to follow, but I disliked the fact that everybody looked more or less the same. I used to sew things onto my clothing so I was still following the rules about what you could wear at school, but managed to look slightly different. So many people just want to fit in these days, for obvious reasons. But I believe we're all starting to dress in similar ways. My clothes allow people to express parts of their personality that they haven't necessarily shown before, and so their unique character can be revealed.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 5\nYou hear a student talking about a project he has done on bees.\n[pause]\ntone\nBoy: I'd like to tell you about a very unusual kind of bee that's found in parts of North and Central America. It's one of several types of nesting bee, which lay their eggs beneath the ground and then head out to search for nectar. This process usually takes place in environments with plenty of flowers, to ensure a reliable supply of food. However, a colony has now been found in the ashes of an active volcano. The area is known for temperatures which can get as high as 42 degrees centigrade. You really wonder how the bees manage to live in this inhospitable place, where little other life is found.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 6\nYou hear a girl talking about giving up social media for two weeks.\n[pause]\ntone\nGirl: I took part in a class research project. I had to give up social media for two whole weeks. I imagined I'd find it far too challenging – I certainly didn't think my ability to concentrate would improve so much. Of course, there were moments when it would've been easy to give in to temptation and sneak a look at my phone. After all, no one was checking up on me. And it was obvious nobody believed me when I said I didn't cope too badly without social media, but I didn't care. So I read news websites instead, and realised how much I could learn from them. I've stuck with them ever since.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 7\nYou hear a teacher telling his students about some research into learning and memory.\n[pause]\ntone\nMan: Let me tell you about some recent psychological research. You might find it useful. So, the psychologists gave students a list of words for easy-to-draw objects such as 'apple' or 'balloon'. After the researcher called out each word, some students had to write it repeatedly, while others were asked to sketch a picture to represent it. Then the students were given an additional unrelated task while background music was played. After that, the students had 60 seconds to write down all the words they could come up with from the first assignment. Those who'd illustrated the words did a much better job of remembering them than those who'd written them multiple times.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 8\nYou hear a girl talking about the choir she sings in.\n[pause]\ntone\nGirl: I'm a member of a choir and, recently, we performed at the city university – an amazing event. It was to mark the fact that the main building was actually 500 years old. And, for the first time ever, we got to sing with an adult choir – very scary, but we were inspired by the example they set, and I was amazed that they actually make a living out of doing it. We were nervous we wouldn't understand their music director's instructions, but we'd had several practice classes with him beforehand. He wasn't particularly impressed with our singing, actually, but apparently we did alright on the day.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nThat is the end of Part One.\nNow turn to Part Two.\n[pause]",
            "explanation": "官方答案为 B：她介意的是这次旅行变了味——本想轻松游玩，结果全家变成比拼\"谁最棒\"的竞争，她明确表示不喜欢，即希望旅行别变得这么好胜。原文：\"it eventually developed into them all seeing who could be the best at everything. I really wasn't keen on that.\""
