        "transcript": "You will hear an interview with a school student from Scotland, called Jake Dawson, who is talking about cycling in the snow in a town in Finland. For questions 24 to 30, choose the best answer (A, B or C).\nYou now have one minute to look at Part Four.\n[pause]\ntone\nInterviewer: Today I'm with sixteen year old school student Jake Dawson, from Scotland, who's just spent part of the winter with relatives in Finland. Jake, welcome! Did you find Finland very different from Scotland?\nJake: Well, one thing about where I was in northern Finland was that it was dark during the day, and although it isn't always bright sunshine in Scotland in the winter months, that did take some getting used to. The public buildings reminded me of where I come from in Scotland, though – something really impressive about their style. And the streets were always full of activity – the weather didn't seem to put people off, which is hardly the case in my hometown.\nInterviewer: Do you go out in the snow in Scotland?\nJake: It's not that I don't go out in snowy weather in Scotland, but I travel around an awful lot by car when I'm there – I did in Finland too. But instead of complaining as I usually do back home, I was able to take in the stunning landscape, and see just how much the snow was a part of that. When I go out in the snow in Scotland, it's a case of having to, for studying and things, not for enjoyment.\nInterviewer: And in Finland, you cycled in the snow, which you hadn't done before.\nJake: Yes, back home it's not something people do when it snows! The number of cyclists really thins out then. But to deal with the ice on the roads and make sure you're safe, what you really need is a mountain bike with thick tyres and good brakes – that isn't too difficult to get hold of actually. The thing is we all just assume the temperature's so low that we'll freeze. But in Finland they just carry on as normal.\nInterviewer: What made cycling in the snow in Finland special?\nJake: Well, it'd be going too far to say drivers kept out of our way and made allowances for us, but they were never actually aggressive. Getting around in the snow was less tricky than at home, though – there was definitely more done to make sure routes for cyclists could be used at all times – although I did take a deep breath when I had to deal with some of the major crossroads – I'd have been grateful for underpasses for cyclists to use.\nInterviewer: And didn't you cycle across a frozen river?\nJake: Yes! It was so cold that the ice was really thick. Some people were lowering their bikes down the bank and cycling across – they obviously thought it was safe – my cousin thought so too. I was pretty reluctant to follow him, but knew I'd never have another chance to do it. So I set off – but then part of the way there I just had to turn back. It looked really dark under that ice!\nInterviewer: Did you ever have any accidents while you were cycling in the snow?\nJake: Well, I'd been warned that it's really easy to lose your sense of direction in the snow and I'd been told to pay extra attention to road signs, and also to concentrate on not sliding out of control and letting the bike slip out from under me. But one time that's exactly what happened because I got too sure of myself. Another cyclist behind me had to brake sharply and then actually ran into me. We were both okay, but it was pretty embarrassing!\nInterviewer: I can imagine! And were you still keen to cycle when you got back to Scotland?\nJake: Sure! In Finland, I'd been taught that in snow, you should keep your weight back, stay relaxed and not speed, so I made up my mind to carry on doing that back home. I'd recently bought a top-of-the-range bike so at least I know how to ride it properly in bad weather. Some of the guys I go to college with are thinking about starting to cycle there next term to save money, so it might be good to do that.\nInterviewer: Well, thanks, Jake for ...\n[pause]\nNow you will hear Part Four again.\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nThat is the end of Part Four.\nThere will now be a pause of five minutes for you to copy your answers onto the separate answer sheet. Be sure to follow the numbering of all the questions. I shall remind you when there is one minute left, so that you are sure to finish in time.\n[pause – four minutes]\nYou have one more minute left.\n[pause – one minute]\nThat is the end of the test. Please stop now. Your supervisor will now collect all the question papers and answer sheets."
