            "transcript": "Test Three.\nI am going to give you the instructions for this test. I shall introduce each part of the test and give you time to look at the questions. At the start of each piece you will hear this sound:\ntone\nYou will hear each piece twice.\nRemember, while you are listening, write your answers on the question paper. You will have five minutes at the end of the test to copy your answers onto the separate answer sheet.\nThere will now be a pause. Please ask any questions now, because you must not speak during the test.\n[pause]\nNow open your question paper and look at Part One.\n[pause]\nYou will hear people talking in eight different situations. For questions 1 to 8, choose the best answer (A, B or C).\nQuestion 1\nYou hear a girl talking about making online videos.\n[pause]\ntone\nGirl: I've always loved watching videos online and that inspired me to make them myself. My idea was to make videos giving advice on fashion, and I did listen to the feedback I got – not necessarily positive – from the people who watched them. I didn't have a particular audience in mind, it was just a fun hobby and I had no big ambitions. Luckily, I had the chance to learn through trial and error. The camera I used wasn't that sophisticated, and I made my films in the evening using a desk light, but you've got to start somewhere! Mastering videos requires patience but I got there by just sticking at it.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 2\nYou hear a girl talking about some photos her dad has taken.\n[pause]\ntone\nBoy: What are these, Kate?\nGirl: Oh, they're just a collection of photos that Dad took of me over a 10-year period – he's an amateur photographer.\nBoy: Really? They're great! Is he intending to put them up somewhere or something?\nGirl: Well, they may well end up in an exhibition, I suppose. But these are really about looking back over what he's done so he can chart his progress, because he's hoping to be good enough to turn professional one day.\nBoy: Still, they're a great way of preserving the past, aren't they?\nGirl: Yeah, and I think he's actually enjoyed looking back at special moments in our family life.\nBoy: I can understand that.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 3\nYou hear a young man talking about being an inventor.\n[pause]\ntone\nMan: I invented a smart phone app that gives the location of school buses on a map, and indicates whether you're on the bus and what time you'll be home. People ask me how I come up with new ideas. Well, it's true that many young inventors study websites featuring the latest trends in technology, and I'm sure doing that can generate ideas. Noticing people's frustrations and problems with their everyday routine, you know identifying practical things that need fixing, is another approach though, and that works for me. Some people are born original and imaginative and can get inspiration out of thin air. But I can't seem to do that.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 4\nYou hear a teacher giving a talk to her class about bats.\n[pause]\ntone\nWoman: Today we're studying bats – did you know there are around 1,300 species in the world! And most of them fly at night – but how do they manage that without crashing into things – or each other? Well, one called a tube-nosed bat, for example, uses its long nose to make a high whistling call at night to detect things like food sources around it, and to prevent collisions. And another, the greater bulldog bat, can catch fish in open water! It sends out sound waves – by making a high-pitched call – and then detects when those sound waves hit an object, such as a fish! ... Okay, now let's see some photographs of ...\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 5\nYou hear a boy talking about going to take part in an archaeology project in Italy.\n[pause]\ntone\nBoy: I'm off to Italy soon – to help out on a site where my dad's working as an archaeologist, excavating an old city. Apparently it was once a really amazing place, but then the whole area was mysteriously abandoned. It'd be cool to be here helping when they finally find out why! So I'm keen to go. And actually, Dad's uncovered some evidence that our ancestors originated from there, so he thinks it'd be a real chance for me to learn more about them. I guess he's right – although Dad also thinks I might want to do archaeology myself one day – we'll have to see about that.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 6\nYou hear a guide at a science museum talking about a new virtual-reality device that visitors can try.\n[pause]\ntone\nMan: You're probably wondering what this is that I'm holding! Well, it's a virtual-reality headset that allows you to see the world exactly how different creatures see it. So, you'll get an insight into how trees and plants might look to different forest animals. You can decide which creature you'd like to be, and that determines which views you'll get – you might be swooping over the treetops, for example, or running around on the ground! And you'll be amazed to find that, according to scientists, many creatures don't see the same colours as we do, so the images of the forest you're in will be totally different from what you might expect.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 7\nYou hear two students talking about some research on listening to music while studying.\n[pause]\ntone\nBoy: I read some research that said listening to music when you're studying helps concentration – a strange subject for research!\nGirl: No stranger than other research that scientists do. I think it'd be quite useful. What did it say?\nBoy: That instrumental music is better than songs when you're studying – not surprising – you can really be put off if you start singing along.\nGirl: Because with songs, you're bound to want to understand what the artist's saying, which is distracting.\nBoy: I like listening to upbeat music when I'm studying, though. It makes me study much longer than I normally would.\nGirl: If I'm enjoying the music too much when I should be working, then it's anything but helpful.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 8\nYou hear a radio presenter talking about something that happens in space.\n[pause]\ntone\nMan: Here's some exciting news for people living in the northern hemisphere and especially people interested in space – today marks the emergence of the black moon. That may sound scary but it's just the name given to the second new moon in a month. As you may or may not know, a new moon occurs when the lunar orbit is between the Earth and the sun. Therefore, the side of the moon lit by the sun is facing away from our planet, making it virtually invisible to the naked eye. It's actually a very rare occurrence. The last black moon was over two years ago, and the next one won't occur for several years.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nThat is the end of Part One.\nNow turn to Part Two.\n[pause]",
            "explanation": "官方答案为 A：男生说跟着歌唱歌会分心，女生补充说听歌总想弄明白歌手在唱什么、很干扰，两人一致认同的是带歌词的音乐对学习的影响，对应 the effect of listening to music with lyrics；对节奏快的音乐两人看法相反。原文：\"Because with songs, you're bound to want to understand what the artist's saying, which is distracting.\""
