        "transcript": "You will hear a girl called Alison giving a class presentation about a holiday she had on a traditional sailing ship. For questions 9 to 18, complete the sentences with a word or short phrase.\nYou now have forty-five seconds to look at Part Two.\n[pause]\ntone\nLast summer, I spent two weeks on a tall ship, travelling round parts of Europe. Tall ships are traditional sailing boats – they're made of wood and have tall masts and large sails. Nowadays, they're used for adventure holidays and teaching sailing skills. I was lucky to be sponsored by an educational charity which was set up to support teenagers who like history – the idea is that helping them take part in certain traditional activities is a good way to do this.\nMy first day on the ship was spent in port, preparing for the voyage. The ship had fourteen permanent crew members and thirty so-called trainees, who were people like me. The captain gave an introductory speech and I was surprised to hear that there were mainly teenagers on the ship, that nobody, apart from him, was over thirty-five, and that most trainees were between fifteen and eighteen. He also stressed that there could be no passengers on board, meaning that everyone was expected to work.\nI already knew I'd be working – I wanted to, in fact. But I must admit, I seriously wondered whether my physical fitness would be up to it. It turned out to be okay actually, and I coped in situations which needed a lot of self-discipline – just having to get up incredibly early, for example.\nThe trainees were divided into teams and each team worked for four hours at a time – from eight a.m. to twelve, and then another four-hour stretch from eight p.m. till midnight. Every couple of days, we'd get a different shift, as it'd be called on land – it's known as a watch when you're out at sea.\nLike most trainees I found it quite difficult to adapt to the timetable – in fact, no one got any sleep at first. Lots of things about being on the ship were new for us – like not having internet access, which was really tough. What I found more challenging than anything, though, was there was no such thing as privacy. For example, I had to share a small cabin with five other people.\nBut, generally, we all got on well. The work we did varied – we cleaned things, helped in a kitchen, and even took control of the wheel, steering the ship sometimes. That made me very nervous, actually – imagine making mistakes with that! Another thing I did was climb the twenty-metre main mast to help fix something. I've always been terrified of heights and I'm proud I managed to do that.\nIt wasn't all work, of course. We went swimming a lot, which was good fun, and it was nice just hanging out with the other trainees, you know, just chatting – we needed that time to relax. Playing cards was the main thing we did when we weren't on duty, though.\nWe stopped at different ports occasionally. We could get off the ship and do things like sightseeing or shopping. There was a market in France, for instance, that many trainees thought was brilliant – I didn't go there because I spent time on a beautiful beach instead – I wouldn't have wanted to miss that. We had a great day in Barcelona too, though unfortunately we arrived just too late to catch the festival that had taken place there.\nBut mostly, we were out at sea, and although we worked a lot, we still saw some amazing things – like hundreds of dolphins and flying fish. Some of the sunsets at sea are impressive too, and I hadn't been prepared for just how many stars are visible at night – there's no light pollution, of course.\nIf any of you fancy going on a tall ship, I could give you details of websites to look at, and also some useful tips. You must have waterproof clothes, for example. I didn't take many, but luckily our ship had a good supply of them. I was short of warm jumpers and trousers too, which was annoying because I had to borrow some from another girl.\nI'd definitely recommend going on a tall ship if you get the chance.\n[pause]\nNow you will hear Part Two again.\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nThat is the end of Part Two.\nNow turn to Part Three.\n[pause]"
