            "speaker": "Speaker 3",
            "answer": "A",
            "transcript": "You will hear five short extracts in which teenagers are talking about making a new friend. For questions 19 to 23, choose from the list (A to H) what each speaker says. Use the letters only once. There are three extra letters which you do not need to use.\nYou now have thirty seconds to look at Part Three.\n[pause]\ntone\nSpeaker 1\nI made a new friend last week. I was just on the bus to school when this girl sat down next to me and asked if I could tell her which stop to get off at for the City Museum. We got chatting. It turned out she'd come from Canada to visit elderly relatives. It seemed to me that she was a bit lonely so I invited her to come along to a concert at my school that evening. We found out we were both into the same kind of music and we were so busy chatting about all the bands we liked that I almost forgot to tell her to get off.\n[pause]\nSpeaker 2\nI've known the kid who lives next door since forever, although we didn't really get on too well. But when we were about eight, his father took us to swimming lessons on Saturday mornings. We both struggled at first because we were so scared of the water, but that kind of brought us together and we've been best mates ever since. That's probably because we've found we share lots of interests. He's cleverer than me but that's never really been a problem for either of us. Mum says the way he talks – you know like he's much older than he really is – reminds her of my cousin. I'm not sure about that.\n[pause]\nSpeaker 3\nAnna's definitely my closest friend. We've shared lots of good and bad moments over the last few years. I broke my wrist last term, for example, and Anna helped me loads, carrying my schoolbooks, that sort of stuff. We were in the same class at school and I remember thinking when I met her that she looked a bit boring, so I wasn't too friendly and I tried to avoid her. I soon realised, though, that she was just shy and actually it turned out that she's got a great sense of humour. She and her family are moving into our street soon so we're going to be neighbours, which'll be great.\n[pause]\nSpeaker 4\nEric's a really interesting person. I got to know him through a project which involved teenagers in different countries working on producing a website together. He was living in Ireland then. He seemed a really nice guy and I wasn't disappointed when we eventually got together face-to-face. The strange thing is, his dad ended up being offered a job in the town where I live so now we're actually going to the same school. Eric found it quite hard moving to a new place and he missed his school back in Ireland, so I'm sure it helped a lot that he already had something in common with me and my mates.\n[pause]\nSpeaker 5\n[pause]\nI started at a new school last year. It was exciting but also very scary to be going somewhere five times the size of my old school. So it was reassuring to find myself sitting next to someone I thought I knew – a girl I'd seen visiting my neighbours. We got talking and it turned out she'd never actually been anywhere near our street – the two of them just looked similar. Anyway, we've become really good friends. We both love horseriding and taking photos, and we're both planning to study medicine. I'm sure we'll never lose touch whatever we end up doing.\n[pause]\nNow you will hear Part Three again.\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nThat is the end of Part Three.\nNow turn to Part Four.\n[pause]",
