        "transcript": "You will hear an interview with a young man called Oliver Stanford, who is talking about how he became a professional gardener. For questions 24 to 30, choose the best answer (A, B or C).\nYou now have one minute to look at Part Four.\n[pause]\ntone\nInterviewer: Today I'm with Oliver Stanford, a gardener in a big gardening company. Oliver, gardening's becoming popular with young people, isn't it? How did you get interested?\nOliver: Well, when I was younger, we lived in the countryside. Mum works as an interior designer and particularly enjoyed doing up the inside of the house – which always looked fantastic! The same couldn't be said about the outside, though! That never got much attention – and it wasn't until I started going to friends' houses, with beautiful gardens, that I saw how awful ours looked in comparison! So I talked to my parents and managed to persuade them to help me set about tidying it up.\nInterviewer: And you decided to become a professional gardener.\nOliver: Yeah, but getting there wasn't straightforward. My parents offered to provide the money for me to go to university – to study horticulture – I didn't take them up on it. I wasn't convinced doing a full-time degree was right for me. I felt I needed hands-on work experience as well, so I worked as an assistant gardener, while studying for my degree part-time. But I'd underestimated how much effort I'd need to put in – loads of assignments! I got there in the end!\nInterviewer: And after graduating, you got your first full-time job at a garden centre.\nOliver: Yes – it was really tough! I started in the winter, outside in freezing glasshouses, putting seeds into pots full of wet soil! My parents had to persuade me not to give up. But I was hoping the experience would help prepare me for the kind of work that lay ahead. Planting seeds then, some time later, selling the fully grown plants, ready to go into someone's garden, gave me great satisfaction.\nInterviewer: And you knew you'd make a successful gardener. Where did that confidence come from?\nOliver: Well, I could see that some gardeners didn't really have the in-depth knowledge of plants that I was building up. And I'm aware of what time of year to do things – what can be left for later and what has to be done now – keeping on top of all that is something I've got used to and am now good at – some gardeners find that difficult, and you won't be any good at the job if you can't do that. So what I want to move on to next is designing gardens that people really appreciate.\nInterviewer: And now you're working with a firm of city gardeners – very different from what you're used to.\nOliver: Yes, the clients are different too! Many seem to have the idea that we can create the perfect garden in just a week, although my boss always refuses to accept their deadlines – and once they understand why, they're fine about it. I did think we'd have problems with access to clients' gardens, you know, because we need to move quite large machinery in there for some jobs we do. I haven't had any issues with that, though.\nInterviewer: Are there any jobs you haven't liked doing?\nOliver: There was one. The house owners were completely changing their garden. The grass had already been taken up and big stones put down, which I didn't think were suitable. Then they asked us to get rid of some hedges that had grown right round the garden. I wasn't keen because they were a habitat for birds and other creatures. Anyway, we did it, but there wasn't much that we could plant instead, as the soil there was such poor quality.\nInterviewer: But you really love the work, don't you?\nOliver: Yes – it's great to work outside with other gardeners, even if they're not as into getting their hands dirty and growing stuff as I am! The real appeal for me is that we can arrive in a garden, which is completely overgrown, and we leave it looking spectacular. I'm actually looking forward to getting a flat with a small garden myself – my salary isn't quite sufficient to stretch to that at the moment – but I'm sure that'll change!\nInterviewer: I'm sure it will! Thanks, Oliver.\n[pause]\nNow you will hear Part Four again.\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nThat is the end of Part Four.\nThere will now be a pause of five minutes for you to copy your answers onto the separate answer sheet. Be sure to follow the numbering of all the questions. I shall remind you when there is one minute left, so that you are sure to finish in time.\n[pause – four minutes]\nYou have one more minute left.\n[pause – one minute]\nThat is the end of the test. Please stop now. Your supervisor will now collect all the question papers and answer sheets."
