            "answer": 1,
            "transcript": "Test Four.\nI am going to give you the instructions for this test. I shall introduce each part of the test and give you time to look at the questions. At the start of each piece you will hear this sound:\ntone\nYou will hear each piece twice.\nRemember, while you are listening, write your answers on the question paper. You will have five minutes at the end of the test to copy your answers onto the separate answer sheet.\nThere will now be a pause. Please ask any questions now, because you must not speak during the test.\n[pause]\nNow open your question paper and look at Part One.\n[pause]\nYou will hear people talking in eight different situations. For questions 1 to 8, choose the best answer (A, B or C).\nQuestion 1\nYou hear a music teacher talking to members of a school choir.\n[pause]\ntone\nWoman: Okay, I think today's practice went quite well, but remember that the concert is only two weeks away and you could sound a lot better. We've been practising these songs for over a month now, and I've told you to learn them off by heart – which you've done. Each of you needs to be aware of how your voice fits in with the overall sound, and my feeling today was that you're more sensitive towards each other than you were. What needs to come across is the feeling that you're putting everything into these songs. They currently sound quite flat, so there's still work to do there.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 2\nYou hear two friends discussing a presentation given by a writer.\n[pause]\ntone\nBoy: Did you enjoy yesterday's presentation?\nGirl: Well, I don't know that writer's work, but I love doing creative writing – it was interesting to hear about some of the things she does to help her develop her stories ...\nBoy: Like how she listens to people's conversations to get the language right.\nGirl: Yeah, but she doesn't include teenage slang – that's really difficult for someone older to do well. I was impressed at how much effort she makes to get to know real people who she then puts into her books – I'd always imagined writers spend their lives on their laptops trying to work out how their imaginary characters will think and act.\nBoy: Yeah, me too.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 3\nYou hear a girl talking about a cycle race she took part in.\n[pause]\ntone\nGirl: I enjoy competitive cycling on off-road tracks, and I've won several races, so it's hardly surprising that everyone just thought I'd win again. In fact, I didn't – I came second – and that was down to me underestimating how muddy the route was going to be. Afterwards, some people criticised me, for being too sure I'd win. Okay – it's true that I'm always quite sure of myself in races – I never assume I can't be beaten though. And I don't have regrets about the amount of practice I did beforehand, because I'd done what I generally do, and any more would've been too much – well that's what I think anyway.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 4\nYou hear a radio item about an art competition in South Korea.\n[pause]\ntone\nMan: The streets of Seoul in South Korea are usually full of activity. But that changes during the extremely wet monsoon season, which keeps people indoors. However, a recent art competition aimed to change this. Artists were encouraged to paint large pieces of artwork, called murals, in different places round the city. The only problem? They had to use hydrochromic paint – only visible when it comes into contact with water. So, to see the paintings, residents had to go outside during the wet weather. The artists who came up with the idea won two of the world's most important prizes, though it's unclear whether residents responded well to this unusual way of creating interest in art.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 5\nYou hear two friends talking about a teenage boy who won a surfing competition.\n[pause]\ntone\nGirl: That TV programme last night about the surfing competition was amazing, wasn't it? I mean, what makes someone decide to enter a competition like that when they're only seventeen!\nBoy: Well not many kids like us get that sort of opportunity. I wish I had the chance to do something like that.\nGirl: And I'd be really proud if I'd managed to beat all those adults and win – it was strange he didn't seem that bothered about it.\nBoy: I thought he'd be making much more of it than he did. But it was certainly a brave thing for him to do.\nGirl: Some people don't see the dangers though, do they?\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 6\nYou hear a man talking about a problem some turtles had trying to reach a wildlife park in Japan.\n[pause]\ntone\nMan: Every summer, hundreds of turtles crawl to a popular Japanese wildlife park from the nearby ocean in order to find food and shelter. Unfortunately, in the past, some turtles used to get caught between railway tracks which run between the ocean and the park. The turtles were unable to find a safe way out in any direction, so park officials and railway engineers got together to dig small U-shaped ditches close to the tracks to provide safe places for the creatures to remain in until they were found. Since then, railway workers have rescued ten turtles and delivered them safely to their new home in the wildlife park.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 7\nYou hear a teacher telling his class about an 'environment day' the school is having.\n[pause]\ntone\nMan: You all know the school is doing something to mark environment day next month. This is a day when we all think more carefully about environmental issues and about improving our local environment. In other years we've acted on suggestions you've come up with, like picking up litter from the school grounds, and teachers have also asked you to spend time checking websites for detailed information on endangered species and their habitats. These are interesting and worthwhile things to do. What we're looking for this time, though, is to do some long-term good, for example by planting trees. So if any of you would like to volunteer to ...\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nQuestion 8\nYou hear a man talking about an extreme marathon he took part in.\n[pause]\ntone\nMan: Last spring I competed, in the US, in what's called an ultra-marathon. It's a one-hundred-and-sixty kilometre race that only one thousand one hundred people have ever attempted – of those only fourteen have actually managed to complete the course. These statistics don't make you feel too confident as you set off, I can tell you! The route itself is extraordinarily demanding – the unmarked, rocky tracks are dangerous and running at altitude's tough – completing the race has been compared to climbing Mount Everest twice! Then there's the fact that no medical help is provided and there are only two water-filling stations along the route. That's what did it for me in the end.\n[pause]\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nThat is the end of Part One.\nNow turn to Part Two.\n[pause]",
