        "transcript": "You will hear an interview with a teenager called Tina Barker, who is talking about a local archaeology project she took part in last summer. For questions 24 to 30, choose the best answer (A, B or C).\nYou now have one minute to look at Part Four.\n[pause]\ntone\nInterviewer: Today, archaeology I'm with Tina Barker, who's been volunteering at a summer community archaeology project which aims to trace the history of her village. But it took time to get started, didn't it, Tina?\nTina: Yes, the idea came from local people who wanted to find out more about the village's history. They settled on the field we're digging in now as a starting point, because an archaeologist came and found evidence that the village had once extended out there. He actually set up the project and looked for volunteers. He stressed to us that it was really important to get suitable tools – so that we didn't destroy things, I guess! Some of the stuff we needed was quite specific to the job we were doing, so it was a while before we tracked it down. But in the meantime, the current owners of the land were impressed by his proposal, and it didn't take them long to decide we could go ahead.\nInterviewer: So what's the group trying to find out?\nTina: Well, the area we're digging in has never been used for growing crops, so it's been undisturbed for centuries. We think underneath we'll find old roads, and the foundations of a 500-year-old building that we believe was in the area, though we're not too sure what the purpose of it was! The real mystery for me is why the entire population just left the area at one point, for no apparent reason. So I've been helping to look for clues in the village births and deaths records – like being a detective, really!\nInterviewer: And you've found something yourself, haven't you?\nTina: Yes, a silver wedding ring. The archaeologist on site thought it was over a hundred years old – but not worth a lot. Anyway, what appealed to me about it was imagining how the woman who lost it must have felt – because it must've meant a lot to her. My mum lost her wedding ring in the park last year, and was really upset. We never found it – but I imagine someone looking in a hundred years' time might dig it up. Who knows, maybe they'll even start looking into who its owner was.\nInterviewer: So there's always an archaeologist on site then?\nTina: Yes, he's great. He's explained how much we can learn from just the tiniest bits of pottery, and how important it is to preserve them. But he'd soon shout at people if he thought they were messing around – I certainly wouldn't want to be shown up like that. And he'd order people off the site if they didn't follow his instructions. But we do try not to cause any damage.\nInterviewer: But I've heard there are cows in the area that could actually do some damage?\nTina: Mmm, most mornings we'll find the cows have knocked down the temporary fence that's been erected, so they're always wandering into the excavation area – they'll walk all over everything in one part of the site and we don't notice till it's too late because we're busy working in another. They seem genuinely curious about what's going on, but the archaeologist says we'll have to fill everything in again if they carry on, and that'd be a real pity.\nInterviewer: But hopefully that won't happen ... So what have you got out of the project?\nTina: Well, everybody agrees it's great to spend time sifting through the soil in detail, knowing you might just discover something about ordinary people living in our village in the past – it makes you forget all your own problems, cos you're so focused on that. And I never saw much point in taking my time over things ... seeing something in the soil, say, and then working carefully around it ... that was a challenge at the outset. I never thought I'd adapt to it like I have.\nInterviewer: And the group have found lots of interesting items. Will they be sold?\nTina: Well, I guess that could get us some money to help us continue! Actually, it'd be great if all these items could stay where they were found so that future residents could find out what life was like in their village, but the archaeologist says most of the stuff will eventually go off to big museums, for everyone to appreciate ... Anyway, it's been a great summer!\nInterviewer: Thanks, Tina!\n[pause]\nNow you will hear Part Four again.\ntone\n[The recording is repeated.]\n[pause]\nThat is the end of Part Four.\nThere will now be a pause of five minutes for you to copy your answers onto the separate answer sheet. Be sure to follow the numbering of all the questions. I shall remind you when there is one minute left, so that you are sure to finish in time.\n[pause – four minutes]\nYou have one more minute left.\n[pause – one minute]\nThat is the end of the test. Please stop now. Your supervisor will now collect all the question papers and answer sheets."
